WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.961 --> 00:00:18.526
10 Dear Daddy Longlegs, Have you ever heard of Michelangelo?

2
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:21.289
I thought I had, but I was wrong.

3
00:00:23.531 --> 00:00:29.296
The other day in English class, the professor said, "Who can tell me something about Michelangelo?"

4
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.202
Just about every girl in the class raised her hand, so I was happy that she called on

5
00:00:36.242 --> 00:00:40.204
me. I spoke up loudly, and everyone could hear me.

6
00:00:40.224 --> 00:00:42.065
"He was an angel."

7
00:00:43.465 --> 00:00:44.346
What a mistake!

8
00:00:45.486 --> 00:00:47.627
The whole class burst out laughing!

9
00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:53.990
Even the professor was trying to hide a smile.

10
00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.011
"Anyone else want to try?"

11
00:00:57.372 --> 00:00:57.792
she said.

12
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:07.284
"Michelangelo was a famous artist who lived in Italy a long time ago," Julia Pendleton said.

13
00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:10.486
"Everybody knows that."

14
00:01:10.506 --> 00:01:15.670
"But he has angel right in the middle of his name!"

15
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.691
I thought, blushing furiously.

16
00:01:18.972 --> 00:01:23.195
The trouble with college is that everyone thinks you already know a lot of things.

17
00:01:25.036 --> 00:01:27.478
But I never learned those things in the orphanage.

18
00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:30.240
It's very embarrassing at times.

19
00:01:32.212 --> 00:01:35.534
Now I keep quiet when the girls talk about something I've never heard of.

20
00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:40.097
Then afterward I look it up in the encyclopedia.

21
00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:45.241
You know, Daddy Longlegs, it isn't the work that's hard at college.

22
00:01:45.681 --> 00:01:46.422
It's the play.

23
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:50.985
Half the time I don't know what the girls are talking about.

24
00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:56.168
I'm a stranger in a foreign country, and I don't understand the language.

25
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.571
It's a miserable feeling.

26
00:02:00.151 --> 00:02:01.292
But I've had it all my life.

27
00:02:03.028 --> 00:02:06.370
In high school the girls would stand in groups and stare at me.

28
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.591
I was odd and different.

29
00:02:10.692 --> 00:02:15.154
I felt like John Grier Home for Orphans was stamped across my forehead.

30
00:02:18.295 --> 00:02:19.576
There was one nice girl.

31
00:02:20.456 --> 00:02:22.937
Sometimes she came over and said something polite.

32
00:02:22.957 --> 00:02:25.978
"How are you today, Jerusha?"

33
00:02:25.998 --> 00:02:28.499
Or "What did you think of the history test, Jerusha?"

34
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.985
Or "I really liked your story when the English teacher read it aloud."

35
00:02:34.365 --> 00:02:37.406
But I hated every one of those high school girls.

36
00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:40.447
And I especially hated the nice one.

37
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:43.748
I knew she only talked to me because she pitied me.

38
00:02:45.388 --> 00:02:47.609
Nobody here knows that I grew up in an orphanage.

39
00:02:48.809 --> 00:02:50.190
Not even Sallie McBride.

40
00:02:53.491 --> 00:02:57.412
One day when we were walking, Sallie said, "You never talk about your family.

41
00:02:58.092 --> 00:02:59.573
Do you have any brothers or sisters?"

42
00:03:00.534 --> 00:03:03.136
"No, I'm an only child," I quickly replied.

43
00:03:03.156 --> 00:03:06.398
"And I've never heard you mention your parents."

44
00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:08.940
"That's because they're dead."

45
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.962
"Oh, I'm so sorry!"

46
00:03:12.342 --> 00:03:12.903
Sallie said.

47
00:03:12.923 --> 00:03:16.245
"I hope I didn't make you feel bad by asking about them."

48
00:03:17.426 --> 00:03:18.227
I shook my head.

49
00:03:18.247 --> 00:03:20.848
"No, they've been dead a long time.

50
00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:25.532
I'm only able to go to college because a kind old gentleman is sending me."

51
00:03:26.568 --> 00:03:28.130
That's mostly true, right?

52
00:03:29.391 --> 00:03:31.012
I just want to be like the other girls.

53
00:03:32.914 --> 00:03:36.277
I just want to forget the John Grier Home and get on with my life.

54
00:03:37.478 --> 00:03:39.340
At least Sallie likes me and is my friend.

55
00:03:41.121 --> 00:03:42.603
I've just been reading over this letter.

56
00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:44.244
It sounds pretty unhappy.

57
00:03:45.305 --> 00:03:49.109
But that's probably because I have an essay due tomorrow and a geometry test.

58
00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.492
And I have a very sneezy cold.

59
00:03:57.384 --> 00:03:58.424
Oh, I almost forgot!

60
00:03:59.285 --> 00:04:02.606
Mrs. Lippett said that you wanted to know everything I'm learning.

61
00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:09.708
Besides English and geometry, I'm taking Latin, French, and biology.

62
00:04:11.268 --> 00:04:15.089
Yesterday the English professor had something nice to say about my latest paper.

63
00:04:15.109 --> 00:04:20.411
"This shows a lot of originality," she said to the whole class.

64
00:04:22.151 --> 00:04:24.692
Which made me feel better about the Michelangelo episode.

65
00:04:25.792 --> 00:04:30.435
The John Grier Home aims to turn out 97 orphans who are all alike.

66
00:04:32.616 --> 00:04:34.997
So it's amazing that I have any originality.

67
00:04:35.837 --> 00:04:39.339
I hope I don't hurt your feelings when I say bad things about the orphanage.

68
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.602
But if I do hurt your feelings, you can stop my allowance.

69
00:04:44.762 --> 00:04:46.783
I guess that's not a polite thing to say.

70
00:04:47.724 --> 00:04:49.845
But you can't expect me to have any manners.

71
00:04:50.845 --> 00:04:52.686
After all, I grew up in an orphanage!

72
00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:58.357
Here's some more news: I'm trying out for the freshman basketball team.

73
00:05:00.238 --> 00:05:02.079
I'm short, but I'm quick and tough.

74
00:05:03.539 --> 00:05:07.201
While the other girls are jumping, I dodge under their feet and grab the ball.

75
00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:09.402
Practicing is fun!

76
00:05:10.922 --> 00:05:14.984
We're outside in the fall air and the trees are all red and yellow.

77
00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:18.425
Everybody is laughing and shouting.

78
00:05:20.266 --> 00:05:24.468
These are the happiest girls I've ever seen—and I'm the happiest of all.

79
00:05:26.352 --> 00:05:26.732
So there.

80
00:05:27.192 --> 00:05:28.973
I've thought of some cheerful things to tell you.

81
00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:31.675
Don't be discouraged when my letters seem sad.

82
00:05:32.655 --> 00:05:36.737
I really love college, and I love you for sending me.

83
00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:40.559
The ten o'clock bell is going to ring in two minutes.

84
00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:44.180
Our day is divided into sections by bells.

85
00:05:45.781 --> 00:05:48.342
We eat and sleep and study by bells.

86
00:05:50.723 --> 00:05:51.423
There goes the bell!

87
00:05:51.804 --> 00:05:52.304
Lights out.

88
00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.263
Observe how seriously I take the rules here.

89
00:05:57.384 --> 00:05:59.226
The John Grier Home has trained me well.

90
00:06:01.869 --> 00:06:04.172
So I've said nice things about the orphanage too!

91
00:06:05.473 --> 00:06:07.435
Sincerely, Jerusha Abbott
